# STTY(1)

## NAME

stty - set terminal options

## SYNOPSIS

| **stty** [ option ... ]

## DESCRIPTION

*Stty* sets certain I/O options on the current output terminal. With no argument, it reports
the current settings of the options. The report goes to the standard error. The option strings
are selected from the following set:

**raw**
: | raw mode input (no erase, kill, interrupt, quit, EOT)

**-raw**
: | negate raw mode

**cooked**
: | same as `-raw`

**cbreak**
: | make each character available to read(2) as received; no erase and kill

**-cbreak**
: | make characters available to *read* only when newline is received

**-nl**
: | allow carriage return for new-line, and output CR-LF for carriage return or new-line

**nl**
: | accept only new-line to end lines

**echo**
: | echo back every character typed

**-echo**
: | do not echo characters

**-tabs**
: | replace tabs by spaces when printing

**tabs**
: | preserve tabs

**ek**
: | reset erase and kill characters back to normal ^? and ^U

**erase** *c*
: | set erase character to *c*. *C* can be of the form `^X` which is interpreted as a `control X`; `^?` is DEL.

**kill** *c*
: set kill character to *c*. `^X` works here also.

That is the whole list, and it is much shorter than v7's.

**Note:** the options this page used to name that describe hardware the BESM-6 has not got were
removed from the program rather than left to fail quietly, on the precedent getty(8) set when
it cut its speed table to one entry. Each was measured against the driver, not assumed:

**50 ... 9600 exta extb 0**
: | the baud rates. A Consul-254 is a parallel typewriter that moves one whole character per instruction; there is no line whose rate could be set. *sg_ispeed* and *sg_ospeed* are stored and handed back by the kernel and read by nothing.

**even -even odd -odd**
: | parity. The Consul line carries eight bits of data end to end.

**cr0-cr3 nl0-nl3 tab0-tab3 ff0 ff1 bs0 bs1**
: | the delays. Nothing on this terminal has a carriage to wait for, and on an eight-bit line a queued byte above 0177 is data and cannot also be a delay count. **-tabs** is the exception and remains: tab expansion is done by column and is live.

**tty33 tty37 vt05 tn300 ti700 tek**
: | terminals that are not this one; each was only a bundle of the delays above.

**lcase -lcase**
: | case mapping. The kernel does implement it, in both directions - and it would undo the driver, whose line is raw because the Consul's own code has no lower-case Latin at all. The text of this system is UTF-8 end to end.

**hup -hup**
: ioctl(2)'s TIOCHPCL sets a bit that nothing in this kernel ever tests. It was this program's
  only *ioctl* call.

**Note: -hup** and **0** were on this page and were never in v7's program.

**Note:** TANDEM, the flow-control flag, is live in the kernel and this program still cannot
set it - v7's had no word for it either.

## DIAGNOSTICS

`stty: not a terminal` if the standard output is not one. v7 ignored gtty(2)'s return and
reported the modes of a zeroed structure instead.

An unrecognised word gives `unknown mode: word` and the remaining options are still applied,
which is v7's behaviour.

## SEE ALSO

ioctl(2), tty(4), getty(8)
